{
  "requirement": "`ToolUsage` holds the cumulative usage for one tool.\n\n```rust,ignore\n#[non_exhaustive]\npub struct ToolUsage {\n    pub calls: u64,\n    pub result_bytes: u64,\n}\n```\n\nTrait implementations\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl Clone for ToolUsage;\nimpl Copy for ToolUsage;\nimpl Debug for ToolUsage;\nimpl Default for ToolUsage;\nimpl Serialize for ToolUsage;\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject `requirements.sdk.graph.running-criteria.ToolUsage` is a declaration contract. It has a `#[non_exhaustive]` struct with public `calls: u64` and `result_bytes: u64` fields. It also lists trait impls for Clone, Copy, Debug, Default and Serialize. The behavioral meaning of the fields is delegated to the refining requirements `ToolUsage.calls`, `ToolUsage.result_bytes`, `ToolUsage.by_name` and `ToolUsage.replay`. Those have their own tests in `agent/tests.rs` and `agent/configured.rs`, and they need no test here.\n\nThe reviewed test is `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]`. It builds rustdoc for the SDK. It then runs `documented_exports`, which calls `check_declaration` on every exported struct page. `md/requirements/sdk/public-api.md` lists `struct ToolUsage` with a link to this requirement's declaration. A missing inventory entry would fail with 'Missing inventory declaration'.\n\nFor `ToolUsage`, `check_declaration` enforces these points:\n- **Trait impls:** `rustdoc.trait_impls(page)` must equal the set parsed from the requirement's second code block. That set is exactly Clone, Copy, Debug, Default and Serialize. A missing derive, such as Copy or Serialize, or an extra impl would fail the test.\n- **Declaration:** the `signature_parts` comparison of the requirement's struct listing against the rustdoc declaration must have no differences in either direction. It would catch a changed field name or type (for example `u64` changed to `u32`), an added or removed public field, and a missing `#[non_exhaustive]`.\n- **Non-exhaustive:** a separate assertion requires `#[non_exhaustive]` on structs that expose public fields.\n- **Source:** `crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph.rs#struct[ToolUsage]` is the implementation. I read it only through the search excerpt, which I used to cross-check. The test derives its expectations from the requirement text.\n\nThe requirement states no serialized form and no Default value semantics beyond the trait list. The test therefore needs nothing further, and no gap is evident. The test is not marked against any other obligation of this subject, and I found no relevant unmarked test.",
